KZ3 patch 2046 (RSX pass 46): exact texture decode diagnostics, direct A8R8G8B8 uploads, separable swizzle tables
and the size-tiered quiet-texture hashing default.
Diagnostics (RSX_PERF=1 + RSX_PERF_PROF=A,B): [perf-redecode|hash|newtex] print exact totals ("total n= bytes= ms")
next to the 128-source tables (which drop every source that arrives once they are full) and a by-shape table
[perf-<tag>-shape] (location, size, format, mips, linear, count, bytes, time, offset range; 48 shapes + other).
[tex-untracked] names the first 64 hash changes with no tracked write; [tex-hash] adds big=, wr-unchanged= (tracked
page flag, no change), dyn-forced= (hashed only because the texture changed once) and argb-direct=.
LD_ARGB_DIRECT (default on, =0 off): pitch-linear A8R8G8B8 textures upload their guest bytes as R8G8B8A8_UNORM with
the RSX remap in the SRV component mapping (output R,G,B,A = comp 1,2,3,0; a select of channel k = resource
component k; op 0/1 = FORCE_0/FORCE_1: decode_texel's default case + remap_comp, bit-exact). KZ3's SPU post chain
rewrites a 1280x720 one (1:0x01310400) and a 160x90 one every frame; the per-texel decode of the big one was 3.4 ms
a frame (run r46-prof: 814 re-decodes, 2,796 ms in the 80 s window).
Swizzle: morton_index(x, y) == morton_index(x, 0) | morton_index(0, y) (each bit's position depends only on lw, lh
and the bit index), so the 2D decode loops (RGBA8, cube, FP16) build one table per axis per level and OR them.
LD_TEX_HASH_SPAN default 4 (was 0) with LD_TEX_HASH_BIG (default 65536, 0 = the untiered 2033 rule): a texture of
at least BIG guest bytes whose content never changed since it was cached and whose hash came back unchanged N
times is re-hashed every N frames; smaller textures and textures that changed once (dyn) every frame. A tracked
write (page bitmap) still forces the next bind's hash in every mode (patch 2033 guarantee); the dyn flag follows
content changes, not page flags (64 KB pages flag neighbours). Host test tools/smoke/test_guest_write.c runs in
three modes (KZ3_GW_TIER=0/1/2) with the new case Q5.

